served\": \"A 2-pin screw terminal J5 is connected directly across the GPIO1 heater-drive net and GND. It is a second output connector on the raw ESP32 GPIO, bypassing the TPS1H200 high-side switch and its protection.\",\n      \"expected\": \"The specification names only J6 as the heater connector, driven through U6 OUT. The heater path should go through the high-side switch, with no undocumented connector on the MCU GPIO net.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"4\\\") (name \\\"/Microcontroller/HEATER\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\") ... (node (ref \\\"J5\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"Pin_2\\\") ... (node (ref \\\"U1\\\") (pin \\\"13\\\")\"},\n        {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"Drives a single **heater output** net through a smart high-side switch (`U6`, TPS1H200AQDGNRQ1) switched from the ESP32's GPIO1\"}\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"If a heater or load is connected to J5, the GPIO1 pin would source the load current directly, risking damage to the MCU pin and defeating the switch's over-current protection.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.2\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The core power, thermocouple SPI and heater-switch wiring matches the specification. Two undocumented items in the Microcontroller sheet look suspicious: a fan-tach diode (D3) coupled to the ESP32 EN net, and an extra screw terminal (J5) wired directly onto the GPIO1 heater drive. Both are low-to-moderate confidence.\"\n}",
